Command Code: keep mid-turn sends, keep Stop a clean abort, and never drop a
message silently.

Upstream PR #10861 rejects a `sendTurn` that arrives while a turn is running
("a turn is already running for this thread"). Every other provider adapter
treats that as a steer, and T3 clients send mid-turn messages on purpose, so
the message is dropped and the thread records a turn-start failure (observed
2026-09-16 while a long turn was in flight). Its interrupt path is broken too:
a child killed by a signal never reports an exit code, the Node spawner fails
the wait with a PlatformError, and the turn surfaces as a provider process
failure instead of an abort — which is what Stop hits.

Command Code headless mode takes one prompt per `-p` process, so a steer cannot
reach the live child. The adapter now queues it on the active run and runs it as
the same turn once that process exits, resuming the same Command Code session —
no new `turn.started`, one `turn.completed` when the queue is empty. The queue
check and the release share a single update so a steer can never land on a run
that will not drain again. A missing exit code is folded back into the exit-code
decision so an interrupt stays an abort. When a turn ends early (failed or
stopped) the queue dies with it, so the run names the undelivered messages in
its error text and abort reason instead of leaving them looking delivered.

